## External dependencies

Colophon vendors and redistributes **no third-party code**. Nothing here is
copied from another project, and there are no pip or npm dependencies at
runtime — the Python is standard-library only by design, the JavaScript is a
plain ES5 subset, and the QML uses only components the Omarchy shell already
provides. The MIT grant above therefore covers the entire contents of this
repository.

What Colophon does depend on is software already installed on the machine, which
it invokes or reads. Each remains under its own license, held by its own authors:

| Dependency | Arch package | How Colophon uses it | License |
|---|---|---|---|
| Ollama | `ollama` | The server itself. Read and written over its local HTTP API; `ollama --version` is the client-version fallback when the server is down | MIT |
| systemd (`systemctl`) | `systemd` | Reads `ollama.service` state, and runs `start`, `stop`, `restart`, `enable`, `disable` on it | LGPL-2.1-or-later |
| Python 3 | `python` | Runs the collector and action scripts | PSF-2.0 |
| libnotify (`notify-send`) | `libnotify` | The "service died unexpectedly" desktop notification | LGPL-2.1-or-later |
| Omarchy shell (Quickshell) | — | Hosts the widget; supplies the UI components and the polkit authentication agent | See the Omarchy project |

Colophon also reads Ollama's on-disk model manifests and blobs under
`/var/lib/ollama`, to list installed models and to show the parameters each one
declares. It writes nothing there — the collector and action scripts make no
filesystem writes at all. The parameter editor changes a model instead by asking
the running server to do it, over that same local HTTP API: `POST /api/create`
carrying only the parameters you edited, which the server applies additively —
everything else in the model's definition survives untouched. The resulting
write is the server's own. Colophon never pulls or deletes a model.

**Privilege.** Colophon installs no root-owned file and ships no polkit rule or
sudoers drop-in. Privileged `systemctl` verbs authorize interactively through the
Omarchy shell's own polkit agent, so every start, stop, restart, enable, and
disable raises an authentication prompt. See the README for what this means in
use.

License identifiers above are stated for the dependency as packaged on Arch at
the time of writing and are informational; each project's own license file
governs.
